{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, switch both bidirectional Transformer blocks from pre-norm to post-norm (norm_first=False), retaining the final LayerNorm. Normalizing each residual sum may improve conditioning when normalized real-item embeddings interact with the unconstrained mask embedding. Keep dimensions, features, embedding parameterization, attention connectivity, readout, loss and optimizer fixed. Preserve masked-target training and appended-mask inference; ensure the predictor writer reconstructs the same architecture.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, reduce training-frequency input modulation from 0.02 to 0.01: scale real-item inputs by 1 + 0.01*tanh(standardized log1p training counts). The improvement from 0.04 to 0.02 motivates testing a weaker frequency signal while retaining variation. Keep padding and mask scales at 1. Preserve embeddings, topology, history selection, readout, loss and optimizer; save the scales in each category's single checkpoint.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, jointly set MAXLEN=64 and batch size=256, retaining width 64, two blocks, two heads, learning rate 7e-4 and dropout 0.1. Shorter sequences reduce padded attention work because history p95 is at most 39; larger batches provide more masked targets per update and may reduce gradient noise. Preserve feature definitions, embedding tying, interaction/readout formulas, loss and optimizer. Keep seed 42, 200 batches per epoch and the 200-epoch cap; propagate dimensions through checkpoint metadata and prediction.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, change only masked-item cross-entropy temperature from 0.8 to 0.7, retaining uniform masked-target weighting and zero label smoothing. Since temperature 1.0 regressed in the supplied observation, modest sharpening is a plausible test for stronger discrimination with normalized tied item embeddings. Preserve sampling, features, embeddings, topology, scoring, appended-mask inference, optimizer, scheduler and batch/epoch settings."
}
